{"results":[{"url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/De1uTYmjdnE/","final_url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/De1uTYmjdnE/","title":"KPCW Radio on Instagram: \"SALT LAKE TRIBUNE -- GOP lawmakers who claimed there’s been widespread voter fraud in Utah demanded a security study of the state’s mail ballots. The Wednesday reveal from researchers: Utah’s system works — and neither “in-person or mail voting is inherently more secure.”\n\nThe two approaches ultimately “remain subject to many of the same risks,” Utah Valley University researchers determined after analyzing election systems in Utah and five other states.\n\nThe taxpayer-funded, $100,000 study was commissioned by lawmakers earlier this year as part of a last-minute compromise to preserve the state’s vote-by-mail election system against a House GOP push to gut it. The results come as political attacks on mail voting continue both in the Beehive State and nationwide.\n\nRead more from the Salt Lake Tribune at KPCW.org. 🔗Link in bio.\"","description":"SALT LAKE TRIBUNE -- GOP lawmakers who claimed there’s been widespread voter fraud in Utah demanded a security study of the state’s mail ballots. The Wednesday reveal from researchers: Utah’s system works — and neither “in-person or mail voting is inherently more secure.”\n\nThe two approaches ultimately “remain subject to many of the same risks,” Utah Valley University researchers determined after analyzing election systems in Utah and five other states.\n\nThe taxpayer-funded, $100,000 study was commissioned by lawmakers earlier this year as part of a last-minute compromise to preserve the state’s vote-by-mail election system against a House GOP push to gut it. The results come as political attacks on mail voting continue both in the Beehive State and nationwide.\n\nRead more from the Salt Lake Tribune at KPCW.org. 🔗Link in bio. - @kpcwradio on October 23, 2026","language":"en","text":"SALT LAKE TRIBUNE -- GOP lawmakers who claimed there’s been widespread voter fraud in Utah demanded a security study of the state’s mail ballots. The Wednesday reveal from researchers: Utah’s system works — and neither “in-person or mail voting is inherently more secure.”  \n  \nThe two approaches ultimately “remain subject to many of the same risks,” Utah Valley University researchers determined after analyzing election systems in Utah and five other states.  \n  \nThe taxpayer-funded, $100,000 study was commissioned by lawmakers earlier this year as part of a last-minute compromise to preserve the state’s vote-by-mail election system against a House GOP push to gut it. The results come as political attacks on mail voting continue both in the Beehive State and nationwide.  \n  \nRead more from the Salt Lake Tribune at KPCW.org. 🔗Link in bio.\n\nThe two approaches ultimately “remain subject to many of the same risks,” Utah Valley University researchers determined after analyzing election systems in Utah and five other states.\n\nThe taxpayer-funded, $100,000 study was commissioned by lawmakers earlier this year as part of a last-minute compromise to preserve the state’s vote-by-mail election system against a House GOP push to gut it. The results come as political attacks on mail voting continue both in the Beehive State and nationwide.\n\nRead more from the Salt Lake Tribune at KPCW.org. 🔗Link in bio.\n\nWaste of our money to discover there’s nothing wrong\n\nReply\n\nWant a Utahn Supreme Court Justice held accountable?   \nFile a complaint with https://jcc.utah.gov/complaint-form/  \nCase name League of Women Voters of Utah, et al. v. Utah State Legislature  \nCase # is 20260365-SC  \n  \nReason for complaint: Menssen’s name appeared on a publicly available list of more than 161,000 Utahns who signed the petition circulated by the Utah Republican Party. Menssen signed the petition on Feb. 12. He now sits on the court deciding the very lawsuit that seeks to enforce Prop 4, and he has already heard arguments in the case. When a judge has personally signed on to one side of a fight, the public has every reason to question his impartiality.  \n  \nCorroborating: Des News, SLTribune, Utah Political Watch, Utah News Dispatch\n\nFile a complaint with https://jcc.utah.gov/complaint-form/\n\nCase name League of Women Voters of Utah, et al. v. Utah State Legislature\n\nCase # is 20260365-SC\n\nReason for complaint: Menssen’s name appeared on a publicly available list of more than 161,000 Utahns who signed the petition circulated by the Utah Republican Party. Menssen signed the petition on Feb. 12. He now sits on the court deciding the very lawsuit that seeks to enforce Prop 4, and he has already heard arguments in the case. When a judge has personally signed on to one side of a fight, the public has every reason to question his impartiality.\n\nCorroborating: Des News, SLTribune, Utah Political Watch, Utah News Dispatch\n\n1 like\n\nReply\n\nProofread!\n\n3 likes\n\nReply\n\nLog in to like or comment.","author":null,"published_date":"2026-10-23T14:00:54.000Z","latency_ms":5831.687580997823,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}